ne graph showing the percentage of U.S. female teens aged 15-19 years using moderately effective and least effective contraceptive methods, compared with long-acting reversible contraception (LARC), among those seeking contraceptive services at Title X service sites during 2005-2013. The percentage who adopted or continued use of LARC at their last service site visit increased from 0.4% in 2005 to 7.1% in 2013.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":501,"renderedHeight":291,"naturalWidth":501,"naturalHeight":291,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 432 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":9346},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m6413a6f2.gif","alt":"The figure is a stacked bar chart showing the percentage of U.S. female teens aged 15-19 years using long-acting reversible contraception (LARC) among those seeking contraceptive services at Title X service sites, by LARC type during 2005-2013. From 2005 to 2013, use of intrauterine devices (IUDs) increased from 3,685 (0.4%) to 17,349 (2.8%), and use of implants increased from 427 (0.04%) to 26,347 (4.3%). Use of IUDs was more prevalent than use of implants during 2005-2011 but was surpassed by implants in 2012 and 2013.","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":501,"renderedHeight":349,"naturalWidth":501,"naturalHeight":349,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 526 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":10094},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/preview/mmwrhtml/figures/m6413a6f3.gif","alt":"The figure is a horizontal bar chart showing the percentage of U.S. female teens aged 15-19 years using long-acting reversible contraception (LARC) among those seeking contraceptive services at Title X service sites, by state in 2013. Colorado had the highest percentage of teen clients using LARC (25.8%), followed by Alaska (19.6%); the lowest percentage was in Indiana (1.5%), and Mississippi (0.7%).","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":832,"renderedHeight":701,"naturalWidth":832,"naturalHeight":701,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"TOO_LONG","altReason":"alt text is 403 characters — consider a caption or description","bytes":22938},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_pdf.gif","alt":"Adobe PDF file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":6058},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_word.gif","alt":"Microsoft Word file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":1087},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_excel.gif","alt":"Microsoft Excel file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":644},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/TemplatePackage/images/icon_ppt.gif","alt":"Microsoft PowerPoint file","hasAlt":true,"isDecorative":false,"isMissingAlt":false,"ariaHidden":false,"rolePresentation":false,"renderedWidth":16,"renderedHeight":16,"naturalWidth":16,"naturalHeight":16,"loading":null,"decoding":null,"altVerdict":"GOOD","altReason":"present, no red flags","bytes":676},{"src":"https://www.cdc.gov/